---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumb from '../../components/Breadcrumb.astro';

const title = "Battery Industry Catch-up — Weeks 41–46, 2026 | Battery Calculators";
const description = "Catch-up for 5 Oct–15 Nov 2026: China’s hybrid spot-market draft, Huntly’s 200 MWh battery, BNEF’s higher forecast, Portugal’s grid-forming rule, DOE BattMan, China’s 167 GW fleet, India’s 92 GWh fund, and CATL Tener Sodium.";
const canonical = "https://batterycalculators.com/news/battery-industry-catch-up-2026-weeks-41-46";
const publishDate = "2026-11-20";
const lastUpdated = "2026-11-20";
const readingTime = 5;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'News', href: '/news' },
  { label: 'Weeks 41–46, 2026' },
];
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  schema="webApplication"
  schemaName="Battery Industry Catch-up — Weeks 41–46, 2026"
  schemaDescription={description}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">

    <!-- Header -->
    <div class="mb-12 border-b border-hairline pb-8">
      <a href="/news" class="text-label-uppercase text-xs text-muted hover:text-primary transition-colors mb-4 inline-block">&larr; Back to News</a>
      <Breadcrumb items={breadcrumbs} />
      <span class="text-label-uppercase text-m-blue-light text-xs block mb-4">CATCH-UP</span>
      <h1 class="text-display-md text-primary mb-4">Battery Industry Catch-up — Weeks 41–46, 2026 (5 Oct–15 Nov)</h1>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 text-caption text-muted mb-6">
        <span>5 Oct–15 Nov 2026</span>
        <span>{readingTime} min read</span>
      </div>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light">
        This is a single catch-up digest for the weeks missed after Battery Industry Weekly — Week 40 (28 Sep–4 Oct 2026). It covers ISO weeks 41–46 (5 October through 15 November 2026) and is not a substitute for the weekly cadence going forward.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Story 1 -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">China proposes joint spot-market bidding for renewables and on-site storage</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">19 October 2026.</strong> China’s National Development and Reform Commission released a draft revision of the Basic Rules for Electricity Spot Markets, open for comment through 18 November, with a proposed start date of 1 January 2027. <a href="https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/22/china-outlines-new-spot-market-rules-supporting-renewable-energy-and-storage-integration/" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PV Magazine</a> reports that Article 8 would let a renewable plant and its co-located battery bid, clear, dispatch and settle as one participant. Bidding formats, metering and settlement remain provincial — usually where hybrid-plant design is won or lost.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Story 2 -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">Genesis opens a 100 MW / 200 MWh battery at Huntly</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">15 October 2026.</strong> Genesis Energy opened its first grid-scale battery at Huntly Power Station in Waikato, New Zealand: 100 MW / 200 MWh, tied directly to the on-site Transpower substation. Genesis’s <a href="https://www.genesisenergy.co.nz/about/news/genesis-opens-first-battery-at-huntly-power-station" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">14 October release</a> puts the cost at about NZ$130 million, more than NZ$5 million under budget, with 70 Saft units installed by Northpower. <a href="https://www.energy-storage.news/genesis-opens-200mwh-battery-storage-system-at-new-zealands-largest-power-station/" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Energy-Storage.News</a> describes the package as LFP containers with power conversion and controls. A second battery of the same size is approved for 2028 operation.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Story 3 -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">BNEF raises its 2026 storage forecast and flags lithium costs</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">22 October 2026.</strong> BloombergNEF’s H2 2026 Energy Storage Market Outlook, reported by <a href="https://www.energy-storage.news/global-energy-storage-deployments-will-see-45-year-on-year-growth-surge-in-2026-bloombergnef/" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Energy-Storage.News</a>, expects 188 GW / 535 GWh of non-pumped-hydro deployments in 2026, 45% above last year’s 112 GW / 307 GWh and above its H1 estimate of 158 GW / 459 GWh. It puts cumulative capacity at 3.1 TW by 2036. BNEF says lithium costs rose tenfold from October 2025 to US$11.5/kWh by May 2026, and that Chinese LFP BESS cell prices more than doubled in that period. It also counts U.S. on-site data-centre storage for the first time, at 4.9 GW / 7.4 GWh in 2026, up from 0.5 GW / 1.4 GWh in 2025.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Story 4 -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">Portugal requires grid-forming and synthetic inertia on new large connections</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">22–23 October 2026.</strong> <a href="https://files.diariodarepublica.pt/1s/2026/10/20502/0000200019.pdf" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Portaria n.º 464-A/2026/1</a>, published 22 October and in force the next day, makes grid-forming operation and synthetic inertia mandatory for storage connecting to Portugal’s transmission network and for Type C plants. Distribution-connected plants must also be able to run grid-following, with the operator choosing the mode. <a href="https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/10/28/portugal-makes-grid-forming-mandatory-for-large-battery-storage-plants/" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PV Magazine</a> and <a href="https://www.energy-storage.news/portugal-makes-grid-forming-mandatory-for-large-scale-bess-and-launches-1gw-tender/" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Energy-Storage.News</a> describe Type D as 45 MW or a transmission connection, and Type C as 10–45 MW. Frequency-response rules apply from 500 kW; while charging, the default underfrequency setting to switch to discharge is 49.2 Hz. The ordinance does not require a reserved state of charge for operator instructions unless a separate regime says so.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Story 5 -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">U.S. DOE publishes a national battery strategy and launches BattMan</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">28 October 2026.</strong> DOE’s Office of Critical Minerals and Energy Innovation issued a <a href="https://www.energy.gov/cmei/articles/does-office-critical-minerals-and-energy-innovation-unveils-new-strategy-and" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Strategy for American Leadership in Batteries</a> and launched the Battery Manufacturing Accelerator (BattMan), led by Pacific Northwest National Laboratory. The stated aims are a fully integrated domestic supply chain by 2030 and U.S. leadership in battery technology and manufacturing by 2035. DOE projects U.S. demand across transport and energy storage to rise from 179 GWh in 2025 to 707 GWh in 2035. The release sets priorities; it does not attach funding amounts or deployment mandates.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Story 6 -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">China’s operational “new-type” storage reaches 167 GW / 435 GWh</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">30 October 2026.</strong> China’s <a href="https://www.nea.gov.cn/20261030/dd2b15f04b92441aa59f839a1a3413ba/c.html" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Energy Administration</a> said operational “new-type” energy storage — electrochemical, compressed-air and other emerging systems, excluding conventional pumped hydro — reached 167 GW / 435 GWh by the end of September 2026, up 63% year on year. Average duration was 2.61 hours, about 0.27 hours longer than a year earlier, and equivalent utilisation was about 915 hours in the first nine months, up about 10 hours. <a href="https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/11/05/chinas-energy-storage-fleet-grows-to-167-gw-as-utilization-improves/" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PV Magazine</a> notes a 15th Five-Year Plan target of 300 GW by 2030.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Story 7 -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">India approves viability-gap funding for 92 GWh of grid-scale storage</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">4 November 2026.</strong> India’s Cabinet approved a viability-gap funding scheme of INR 14,500 crore for FY 2026–27 to 2030–31, covering 92 GWh of as-yet uncommissioned grid-scale storage: 60 GWh pumped hydro, 30 GWh batteries and 2 GWh of alternative technologies, with estimated investment of INR 97,600 crore. The <a href="https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleseDetailm.aspx?PRID=2332474&amp;lang=1&amp;reg=1" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Press Information Bureau</a> says capacity will be allocated by tariff-based competitive bidding, and that battery projects must meet a minimum 40% domestic-content requirement — a bid-eligibility constraint, not only a cost input. The government frames the 92 GWh as 170% of current grid-scale storage, toward a stated need of 411 GWh by 2031–32.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Story 8 -->
    <section class="mb-12 border-t border-hairline pt-12">
      <h2 class="text-title-xl text-primary mb-4">CATL publishes four-hour Tener Sodium specifications</h2>
      <p class="text-body-md text-body font-light mb-4">
        <strong class="text-primary">29 October 2026 (reported 14 November).</strong> At an ATA Insights webinar on 29 October, CATL presented datasheets for Tener Sodium, the four-hour sodium-ion BESS launched in June. <a href="https://www.pv-magazine.com/2026/11/14/clarifying-the-catl-tener-sodium-with-new-specifications-and-availability/" class="text-m-blue-light hover:text-primary transition-colors" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PV Magazine</a> reports a rated block energy of 30+ MWh at up to 0.25P (7.5 MW for four hours), 95% round-trip efficiency at 25°C and 0.25P, and a 70% state-of-health claim after 20 years at one cycle per day. Against its EnerX 0.25P LFP system, CATL claimed a roughly 32% smaller footprint for a 500 MWh layout and 9.2% lower EPC cost, excluding transport, the substation and “DC/AC side” costs. These are vendor figures, not independent tests. CATL said the product is already being delivered in China, with overseas deliveries aimed at summer 2027.
      </p>
    </section>

